      "title": "Abiy Ahmed Calls Ethiopia’s Sea Access Existential, Offers Dialogue at Tana Forum - https://kulu-media.com/",
      "link": "https://kulu-media.com/abiy-ethiopias-sea-access-tana-forum/",
      "date": "Sat, 24 Oct 2026 18:47:53 GMT",
      "image": "https://kulu-media.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/abiy-ahmed-ethiopias-sea-access-tana-forum-2026-1000x600.jpg",
      "content": "Abiy Calls Ethiopia's Sea Access Existential at Tana Forum | Kulu Media Home Latest News Ethiopia Tigray Eritrea Sudan Africa World Politics Economy Finance Business Education More Analysis Entertainment Tech Health Sports English English ትግርኛ አማርኛ Connect with us Search Kulu Media Abiy Ahmed Calls Ethiopia’s Sea Access Existential, Offers Dialogue at Tana Forum Home Latest News Ethiopia Tigray Eritrea Sudan Africa World Politics Economy Finance Business Education More Analysis Entertainment Tech Health Sports English English ትግርኛ አማርኛ Home » News » Ethiopia » Abiy Ahmed Calls Ethiopia’s Sea Access Existential, Offers Dialogue at Tana Forum Ethiopia Abiy Ahmed Calls Ethiopia’s Sea Access Existential, Offers Dialogue at Tana Forum Abiy links Ethiopia’s maritime ambitions to security, sovereignty and regional cooperation. Published 1 day ago on October 24, 2026 By Kulu Media News Desk Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed addresses the 12th Tana High-Level Forum in Bahir Dar on October 24, 2026, discussing Ethiopia's sea access, political dialogue and economic sovereignty. Photo Credit: EBC / YouTube, Screenshot by Kulu Media Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed has described reliable access to the sea as an existential issue for his country, while reaffirming his government’s willingness to pursue dialogue over political and security disputes. Addressing the 12th Tana High-Level Forum on Security in Africa in Bahir Dar, Abiy said Ethiopia’s population of more than 130 million made dependable international trade routes essential to its long-term economic development and national security. “For a nation of more than 130 million people, access to the sea is existential,” he told African leaders and delegates attending the forum. Abiy emphasized that Ethiopia would continue seeking solutions through dialogue and mutual benefit, framing maritime access as part of a broader strategy linking economic sovereignty, regional integration and lasting peace. His remarks came amid heightened tensions in the Horn of Africa, where Ethiopia’s pursuit of greater maritime access has become an increasingly sensitive diplomatic and security issue. Abiy Reaffirms Dialogue Amid Security Challenges The prime minister acknowledged the human consequences of conflict in Ethiopia, saying the effects of violence often extend far beyond the battlefield and continue to affect families long after fighting ends. He said protecting civilians and upholding the constitutional order remained central responsibilities of the government. At the same time, Abiy reiterated his willingness to engage groups prepared to abandon violence and pursue peaceful political dialogue. “We are ready and we will receive them with open arm and open heart,” he said. The remarks come as the Ethiopian National Defense Force (ENDF) and its Tigray Peace Force (TPF) allies continue military operations against TPLF-aligned forces in northern Ethiopia. Federal forces entered Mekelle earlier this month, and the TPLF subsequently confirmed its withdrawal from the regional capital. Abiy did not name the TPLF or specify particular armed organizations when extending his invitation to dialogue. The prime minister also discussed Ethiopia’s national dialogue process, saying recommendations had been submitted to parliament and regional governments. He called for those recommendations to be translated into concrete government decisions and practical measures. Reflecting on African-led mediation, Abiy argued that signing a peace agreement was only the beginning of a longer process requiring implementation, functioning institutions and improved livelihoods. His remarks come as the African Union continues efforts to preserve the peace process in northern Ethiopia, including a recent mediation initiative involving former Nigerian President Olusegun Obasanjo. Ethiopia’s Sea Access and Regional Trade Abiy placed particular emphasis on Ethiopia’s landlocked position, arguing that dependence on routes through neighboring countries created long-term economic and security vulnerabilities. He said Ethiopia would continue pursuing dependable access through negotiations and arrangements intended to benefit all parties. “This concerns our security, our sovereignty and the future of our children,” he said. The prime minister linked Ethiopia’s maritime ambitions to the development of regional transport corridors and implementation of the African Continental Free Trade Area. He argued that efficient cross-border transport could expand market access, support small businesses and reduce opportunities for illicit trade networks. His comments follow renewed regional debate over Red Sea security and maritime access. During a related discussion at the Tana Forum, Ethiopian National Security Adviser Redwan Hussein called for collective Red Sea security , emphasizing cooperation among coastal and landlocked countries. Abiy did not announce a new maritime agreement or identify a specific port arrangement in his speech. Energy Exports and the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam Turning to economic development, Abiy presented the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam as an example of Ethiopia’s efforts to strengthen domestic infrastructure and expand regional economic cooperation. He said the country’s investment in the dam should translate into more reliable electricity, industrial development and improved livelihoods. According to the prime minister, Ethiopian electricity is reaching Kenya, Sudan, Djibouti and Tanzania. He portrayed cross-border energy cooperation as a practical example of regional integration, arguing that shared infrastructure could strengthen economic ties and reduce tensions. The speech did not provide separate figures for electricity exports to each destination. Gold Revenue and Domestic Mineral Processing Abiy also highlighted Ethiopia’s mining industry, saying gold generated approximately $5.6 billion in revenue during the country’s last fiscal year. The figure was presented by the prime minister and was not independently verified in the speech. He said Ethiopia had historically exported much of its gold in raw form but was now seeking to capture more value through domestic processing. Abiy pointed to the opening of a precious metals refinery in Addis Ababa, which he said could serve Ethiopia and neighboring African countries by refining and certifying gold. He argued that African countries should expand mineral processing, technical training and transport infrastructure to create jobs and retain more revenue from their natural resources. The prime minister also called for greater participation by African businesses in investment projects across the continent. Digital Ethiopia 2030 and Artificial Intelligence Technology was another central theme of the address. Abiy said economic sovereignty increasingly depended on countries controlling their own digital infrastructure, data systems and technological standards. He highlighted Ethiopia’s Digital Ethiopia 2030 strategy, including efforts to connect the Fayda digital identification system with payment platforms and public-service data exchange. The prime minister said such systems could improve access to government services while emphasizing the importance of protecting personal information and providing mechanisms for correcting errors. He also announced plans to develop an artificial intelligence university intended to welcome students from across Africa. According to Abiy, the institution would focus on developing technologies capable of addressing practical challenges. The speech did not provide an opening date or detailed funding arrangements for the proposed university. Ethiopia Prepares to Host COP32 Abiy also connected environmental protection and climate adaptation to Africa’s long-term security. He warned that drought and environmental degradation could intensify competition over land and water, potentially contributing to conflict. The prime minister said Ethiopia had planted more than 56 billion seedlings over eight years through its Green Legacy Initiative. He presented the program as part of efforts to protect agricultural land, water resources and rural livelihoods. Abiy also reaffirmed that Ethiopia would host the COP32 United Nations climate conference in 2027. He invited African governments to coordinate their positions ahead of the gathering, particularly on climate adaptation financing and investment in clean energy. The prime minister said African countries should prioritize financing that reaches farmers and pastoralists while supporting energy projects that create jobs. Calls for Stronger African Institutions Abiy concluded by calling for stronger African institutions with adequate financing, professional staffing and broader representation. He questioned whether the African Union’s current staffing adequately reflected the continent’s diversity and argued that regional organizations needed resources and authority appropriate to their responsibilities. He said African governments should contribute more consistently to institutions responsible for addressing shared security and development challenges. Referring to Ethiopia’s traditional practice of elder-led mediation, known as shimglina , Abiy emphasized the importance of trust, reconciliation and keeping commitments. His address placed economic resilience, political dialogue and regional cooperation at the center of Ethiopia’s stated approach to peace and security. The speech also underscored the government’s position that access to the sea remains a strategic national priority, even as Addis Ababa continues to advocate negotiated solutions. The 12th Tana High-Level Forum has also featured calls for stronger African economic independence and regional security cooperation. Ghanaian President John Dramani Mahama urged African governments to strengthen economic sovereignty , expand domestic industries and finance their own peace operations. In a related Bahir Dar security dialogue, Ethiopian National Security Adviser Redwan Hussein called for collective Red Sea security , arguing that landlocked countries should also have a role in protecting regional maritime trade routes. Related Topics: Abiy Ahmed Economic Sovereignty Ethiopia Sea Access Red Sea Tana Forum Up Next Over 14,000 Ethiopians Flee Moyale Fighting Into Kenya Don't Miss Ghana’s President Calls for African Economic Sovereignty to Secure Lasting Peace Continue Reading You may like Ghana’s President Calls for African Economic Sovereignty to Secure Lasting Peace Getachew Reda Says TPLF Is No Longer Organized for Peace Talks, Sets Conditions for Eritrea Dialogue Ethiopia Calls for Collective Red Sea Security as Regional Tensions Rise Ethiopia Ethiopia’s Army Chief Accuses Eritrea, Egypt and Sudan of Backing TPLF Ethiopian army chief alleges foreign backing for TPLF forces amid tensions. Published 48 seconds ago on October 25, 2026 By Kulu Media News Desk Ethiopian National Defense Force Chief of Staff Field Marshal Berhanu Jula speaks during a military gathering. Photo: ENDF Ethiopia’s military chief, Field Marshal Berhanu Jula, has accused Eritrea, Egypt and Sudan of coordinating support for TPLF-aligned forces, alleging that Ethiopian troops intercepted a large shipment of weapons transferred through Eritrea’s Red Sea port of Massawa. The allegations were made on October 25, 2026, during remarks marking the anniversary of the Ethiopian armed forces, according to speech during the 119th Ethiopian National Defense Force day. Field Marshal Berhanu Jula claimed that the Ethiopian National Defense Force (ENDF) had seized a substantial cache of weapons intended for the Tigray People’s Liberation Front (TPLF). He also accused the three neighboring countries of coordinating with the group in efforts to launch a war against Ethiopia. The military chief’s allegations have not been independently verified. No publicly verified inventory of the seized weapons or evidence establishing their alleged route through Massawa was identified in the reporting reviewed. The latest Ethiopia Eritrea TPLF accusations come amid heightened regional tensions and renewed fighting involving federal troops and TPLF-aligned forces in northern Ethiopia. Army Chief Claims Offensive Was Thwarted In a separate account of Berhanu Jula’s October 25 address in Addis Ababa the army chief said Ethiopian forces had thwarted a TPLF offensive across North Wollo, Wag Hemra and Afar. Berhanu Jula alleged that the operation had received external support and said the ENDF would continue defending Ethiopia’s sovereignty. He also rejected attempts to establish a transitional government through armed struggle. The speech remarks suggest that Ethiopian military leaders continue to view the conflict in northern Ethiopia as involving both domestic armed opposition and alleged foreign interference. However, the specific claims concerning foreign military coordination, weapons shipments and the outcome of the alleged offensive remain unverified independently. Accusations Follow Earlier Diplomatic Confrontation The October 25 allegations follow similar accusations made by Jula in late September. In a September 28 report , the Associated Press documented his earlier accusations that Eritrea was financially supporting the TPLF and that Sudan and Egypt could also be involved in backing armed groups opposing the Ethiopian government. Sudan rejected those earlier allegations. According to Sudan Tribune , the Sudanese Armed Forces dismissed the accusations as baseless and maintained that Sudan was committed to international law and good-neighborly relations. Eritrea has also accused Ethiopia of pursuing hostile policies toward its territory, particularly in connection with Ethiopia’s longstanding demand for access to the Red Sea. The competing allegations reflect the deterioration of relations between Ethiopia and Eritrea since their cooperation during the 2020-2022 Tigray war. No independently verified response from Eritrea, Egypt or Sudan specifically addressing Jula’s October 25 Massawa weapons allegation was identified in the sources reviewed. Renewed Fighting Raises Regional Concerns The latest statements come as Ethiopia faces renewed armed confrontation involving TPLF-aligned forces in Tigray and neighboring regions. The conflict has continued despite the November 2022 Pretoria agreement, which formally ended the two-year war between the Ethiopian federal government and TPLF forces. Political disagreements over implementation of the agreement, security arrangements and regional administration have contributed to renewed instability. The TPLF had previously warned of war over unresolved issues surrounding the peace agreement. An Associated Press report published on October 23 described continued tensions between TPLF-aligned military commanders and Tigray’s federally backed caretaker administration. The administration has urged armed fighters to disarm, while a senior TPLF-aligned commander has vowed to continue resisting government forces. The conflict also involves the Tigray Peace Force (TPF), which is aligned with the caretaker administration and operates separately from TPLF-aligned forces. The divisions have complicated efforts to establish a lasting political and security settlement. Earlier this month, renewed fighting in Central Tigray was followed by counteroffensive claims from TPLF-aligned forces. Massawa Allegation Adds New Dimension Jula’s specific reference to the port of Massawa introduces an additional element to Ethiopia’s accusations against Eritrea. Massawa is a major Eritrean port on the Red Sea. The allegation that weapons intended for TPLF-aligned forces passed through the port, if substantiated, would raise further questions about cross-border military supply networks. However, the available reporting does not establish when the alleged shipment occurred, what weapons were involved, who transported them or whether independent observers examined the seized equipment. Those details remain important to evaluating the military chief’s account. The accusations also arise amid wider disagreements over Ethiopia’s pursuit of maritime access. Ethiopian officials have repeatedly described access to the sea as a strategic national priority. Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed recently called sea access existential during the Tana High-Level Forum, while Eritrea has opposed statements it interprets as threatening its territorial sovereignty. A Critical Threats Project assessment published October 8 documented the deterioration in Ethiopia–Eritrea relations and the growing involvement of regional diplomatic actors. Diplomatic Implications Remain Uncertain The October 25 remarks underscore the Ethiopian military leadership’s position that the conflict involving TPLF-aligned forces has a regional dimension. The accusations could further complicate relations between Ethiopia, Eritrea, Sudan and Egypt, which already face disagreements involving security, borders and regional strategic interests. The diplomatic tensions also coincide with military operations near the Eritrean border, where drone strike damage was documented in the northern Tigray town of Rama earlier this month. Whether the latest statements lead to additional diplomatic measures or changes in military activity remains uncertain. For now, the alleged weapons seizure and foreign coordination remain claims by Ethiopia’s military leadership, without publicly available independent evidence establishing their accuracy. Continue Reading Ethiopia Over 14,000 Ethiopians Flee Moyale Fighting Into Kenya Over 14,000 flee Moyale clashes as humanitarian needs surge in Kenya. Published 23 hours ago on October 25, 2026 By Kulu Media News Desk Ethiopians who fled fighting in Moyale gather at a holding area in Kenya on October 23, 2026. Photo: Kenya Red Cross Society More than 14,000 Ethiopians have fled deadly fighting in the southern border town of Moyale into neighboring Kenya, as humanitarian agencies warn that shortages of food, clean water and sanitation facilities are threatening thousands of displaced families. The latest figures, reported on Saturday, October 24, 2026, mark a sharp escalation in the Moyale fighting displacement crisis, which began on October 21 amid tensions involving communities from Ethiopia’s Oromia and Somali regional states. According to Anadolu Agency , Kenya Red Cross officials said more than 10,900 displaced people were sheltering in six temporary camps in the Kenyan border town of Moyale, while approximately 4,000 others were staying with relatives and host families. The reported numbers represent a substantial increase from the 2,500 displaced Ethiopians documented in earlier reports on October 23. The growing number of arrivals has intensified pressure on humanitarian services in the Kenyan border region, where emergency responders are struggling to meet the needs of families fleeing the violence. Kenya Red Cross Warns of Critical Shortages The Kenya Red Cross has appealed for additional humanitarian assistance as displaced families face increasingly difficult living conditions. Malik Adan, the organization’s head of the Upper Eastern region, told reporters that emergency shelters urgently needed more food, drinking water, sanitation supplies and toilet facilities. Humanitarian officials warned that inadequate sanitation could increase the risk of diarrheal diseases, particularly in crowded temporary camps where access to clean water and hygiene facilities is limited. The Kenya Red Cross has distributed tents, blankets, sleeping mats and other essential supplies while providing nutritional assistance to children and breastfeeding mothers. Local authorities have also pledged additional food supplies, including rice and beans, but relief workers remain concerned that available resources may be insufficient for the growing displaced population. The latest displacement figures underscore the rapid deter",
      "summary": "Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed emphasized the critical importance of maritime access for Ethiopia during his address at the 12th Tana High-Level Forum on Security in Africa, held in Bahir Dar. He described reliable sea access as an existential issue for the nation, which has a population exceeding 130 million, linking it to national security and economic development. Abiy reiterated his government's commitment to dialogue in resolving political and security disputes, inviting groups willing to abandon violence to engage in peaceful discussions. He highlighted Ethiopia's landlocked status as a vulnerability, advocating for negotiations to secure dependable trade routes. Abiy also discussed the Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam's role in enhancing regional cooperation and energy exports. He called for stronger African institutions and emphasized the need for economic sovereignty and regional security collaboration. His remarks underscored Ethiopia's strategic focus on maritime access, regional integration, and the importance of addressing environmental challenges as part of a broader peace and security strategy.",
      "page_url": "https://aynnews.net/news/abiy-ahmed-calls-ethiopia-s-sea-access-existential-offers-di-da71745978.html"
    }
